  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The fifth inning was the pivot. Down one, Haase hit his first, then his second, and suddenly they were up by three with the game theirs to lose. They didn't.",
    "what_this_means": "Haase supplied the power, but the real story is that they finally converted runners in scoring position when it mattered. Two of seven RISP situations paid off, which sounds thin until you remember they trailed through four innings and still finished with six runs. The bullpen held after Houser exited. Gage, Peguero, Hentges, and Kilian combining for 2.4 innings of clean work, which is the kind of mechanical perfection that lets a fragile offense breathe. They won by four against the Dodgers in LA, which is exactly the kind of road result that either means something or means nothing depending on whether this team is actually climbing or just bouncing. The win streak reached three. The margin to first stayed at 6.5. Nothing decisive yet, but the pattern is starting to matter more than the single night.",
    "what_to_watch": "Shohei Ohtani takes the mound tonight with a 0.97 ERA. A different kind of challenge than managing a close game. Ray has been sharp over his last four starts at 3.12, which might be just enough if the offense can find one clean inning before the ninth.",
